    "summary": "NYE high‑energy set with fierce Next Time You See Me and soaring Not Fade Away sandwich",
    "blurb": "The December 31 show bursts with electric guitar work on “Next Time You See Me” and a wild “Not Fade Away” → “Goin’ Down The Road Feeling Bad” → “Not Fade Away” sandwich. Jerry’s blistering leads, Keith’s debut piano and Bill’s relentless drumming keep the momentum rolling from the opening “Dancin' In The Street” through an explosive second‑set jam. Excellent soundboard sources capture every nuance of this historic night.",
    "review": "What unfolds at Winterland on New Year's Eve 1971 is a quintessential early‑70s Dead experience – tight, playful, and unapologetically loud. The first set kicks off with a rollicking “Dancin' In The Street” that immediately sets a bright, driving vibe; Jerry’s guitar slides into a ferocious solo on “Next Time You See Me,” while Phil lays down a thunderous bass foundation that fans have called a “Phil bomb.”  The second set launches into the classic “Truckin’” → drums → “The Other One” → “Me And My Uncle” → “The Other One” suite, delivering a sprawling improvisational peak that showcases Bill’s fire‑breathing drumming and Keith’s freshly minted piano textures.  The night caps with a high‑octane “Not Fade Away” sandwich sandwiching “Goin’ Down The Road Feeling Bad,” leading into an exuberant encore of “Casey Jones.”  Across all available recordings the performance feels locked in, and the best soundboard capture (gd1971-12-31.142742.sbd.miller.flac1648) preserves the full band balance with crystal‑clear vocals and punchy instrumentation.",
